HALVED {6,4} AND {4,6} — the dual pair, both cut by a diagonal          2026-08-14, CC
================================================================================================

WHAT THESE TWO BOARDS ARE, AND WHAT THEY ARE NOT

  {4,6} halved   six squares meet at every {4,6} vertex, so each corner is 60 degrees and a diagonal
                 cuts one square into two 30-60-30 triangles. Isosceles, achiral, sides DERIVED from
                 the angles (a hyperbolic triangle is rigid). The direct successor to {4,5} halved:
                 same cut, one valence further along.
  {6,4} halved   four hexagons meet at every {6,4} vertex, so each corner is 90 degrees and the LONG
                 diagonal — vertex to opposite vertex, through the face centre — cuts one hexagon into
                 two 45-90-90-45 QUADRILATERALS. A mirror of the hexagon, so the halves are achiral.
                 Sides DECLARED: a hyperbolic quadrilateral has 2n-3 = 5 degrees of freedom against 4
                 angles, so its angles do not pin it.

  ⚑ CORRECTION TO WHAT I PROPOSED. I offered these as boards that would "extend the parity story to
  another even valence". That was wrong, and I should have caught it before suggesting it. The parity
  argument is about ALTITUDE cuts, whose foot lands at an edge MIDPOINT and forces the neighbour across
  that edge to cut there too; it says a {3,q} vertex can be left uncut only when q is even. BOTH cuts
  here run vertex to vertex, so no edge is halved, there are no half-edges to alternate, and neither
  board touches that argument. What they do test is different and still worth having: how the catalogue
  moves when the CUT is held fixed (a diagonal) and the VALENCE rises — {4,5} halved to {4,6} halved —
  and whether a dual pair behaves alike.

SIDES, AND ONE EXACT COINCIDENCE
  {4,6} half:  S = 1.762747174039087 = arccosh(3)   (the {4,6} edge)
               D = 2.292431669561179 = arccosh(5)   (the diagonal)
  {6,4} half:  S = 1.316957896924817                (the {6,4} edge)
               D = 2.292431669561179                (the long diagonal = 2 x circumradius)
  The two D's are the SAME number to 1e-15, and not by accident: cosh R = cot(pi/p)cot(pi/q) is
  symmetric in p and q, so a dual pair shares a circumradius, and both cuts are 2R. The two boards' cut
  edges are congruent even though nothing else about their tiles is. ({4,5} and {5,4} share 2R =
  1.684964162924016 the same way — visible in the two shipped boards, unremarked until now.)
  No 2:1 ratio on either board, and there should not be: that relation belongs to altitude cuts, where
  the short side is half the base. D/S is 1.300 here and 1.741 there.

ALPHABETS (gen_alphabet.py, both accepted first time)
  hyp-46-half   D=12  angles [1,2,1]    edges S,S,D   4 of 9 adjacent pairs forbidden   49 vertex types
  hyp-64-half   D=8   angles [1,2,2,1]  edges S,S,S,D 6 of 16 forbidden                 33 vertex types
  Both achiral on the (angle, edge) word, so no mirror twin — and that is now a checked statement, not
  an assumption: the test that missed the {5,4} tile yesterday runs on the edge word too.

