THE k=1 QUESTION, AND WHY IT HAS AN ANSWER
  {6,4} has nothing at k=1 and {4,6} has 14. That is not a quirk of the search; it is counting.
  A diagonal puts 2 cut-ENDPOINTS on each face, and pF = qV, so the average vertex is an endpoint of
  2F/V = 2q/p diagonals. A vertex-transitive tiling needs every vertex to sit at that average, so it
  needs 2q/p to be a whole number:
      {4,5}  5/2   no k=1     (and the shipped board starts at k=2 — this EXPLAINS a floor I had
                               previously only observed)
      {6,4}  4/3   no k=1     confirmed: the search returns nothing
      {4,6}  3     k=1 ALLOWED
  Predicted before the {4,6} run finished, and confirmed twice over: 14 tilings at k=1, and in every
  one of the 26 vertex orbits across them the vertex is the endpoint of exactly 3 of its 6 diagonals —
  3 squares cut, 3 whole, nothing else. So the bound is not just satisfied on average, it is tight
  everywhere, which is what the argument actually asserts.
  And the vertex figures are exactly the ones combinatorics allows: 3 cut + 3 uncut squares round a
  6-cycle makes 3 bracelets, and all 3 appear across the 14 tilings, with nothing else —
      XXXOOO  30.30.30.30.30.30.60.60.60   (5 vertex orbits)
      XXOXOO  30.30.30.30.60.30.30.60.60   (20)
      XOXOXO  30.30.60.30.30.60.30.30.60   (1)
  Nothing forced the search to realise all three; it did.

  Note this is a DIFFERENT parity story from {3,7}/{3,8}. That one is about altitude cuts and half
  edges alternating round a vertex. This one is about endpoint counting and needs no half-edge at all.
  For the {4,q} family it does reduce to a parity: 2q/4 is whole iff q is even.

INDEPENDENT CHECKS (the counts have no census to reproduce, so these are what there is)
  * k vs geometry. The pruner assigns k; the certifier counts rneig cycles knowing nothing about it.
    The dart set is chirality-doubled, so a vertex orbit is two cycles unless a mirror identifies them,
    giving k <= vertexOrbits <= 2k. Holds on all 7,453 records across the six boards, 0 violations.
  * Re-canonicalisation. The pruner dedups with its own WL/DFA form. A second canonical form written
    from scratch (BFS from every root over (rneig, glue) with per-dart angle/length labels, minimised
    over both orientations so mirror pairs fold) agrees on every record of all three boards it was run
    against: 1,296 / 15,919 / 61 distinct forms from 1,296 / 15,919 / 61 records, zero duplicates.
    That is the pruner's own job checked by something that shares no code with it.
  * Side lengths. {4,6}'s are DERIVED by the developer from the angles alone and come out arccosh(3)
    and arccosh(5) — matching the {4,6} edge and 2R computed independently from cos(pi/p)/sin(pi/q)
    and cot(pi/p)cot(pi/q) to 1e-15. {6,4}'s are declared, and the face cycle product is what accepts
    them; a wrong declaration fails every record rather than bending the shape.

{4,6} AT k=4 — ENUMERATED, NOT SHIPPED
  The solver produced 1,921,832 raw blocks and 3.4 GB for k=4 alone (against 2,160 blocks for the WHOLE
  {6,4} board). The pruner ran it to completion: 564,906 distinct tilings at k=4 alone, against 15,919 for k<=3
  put together. At the shelf's ~3 KB per quotient it is well
  over a gigabyte of JSON, so it is off the shelf and `HypHalfBoard.dropped` says so. Three claims stay
  apart on every board now: shipped, `emptyKs` (the board has nothing there), `dropped` (we do not ship
  what it has). `lib/tilings/hyp-half.test.ts` checks each k the search covered is claimed exactly once.

