TWO BUGS THIS RUN EXPOSED IN THE SHELF (both pre-existing, both shipped)
  ⚑ lvert AND sizes WERE HARDCODED TO 3. True of every board until {5,4}, which halves into a
    QUADRILATERAL, as does {6,4}. Not cosmetic: `maxTileRadius` reads lvert for the face-size term of the
    client's develop margin and asinh(sinh(l/2)/sin(pi/p)) GROWS with p, so a 4-gon called a 3-gon
    under-reserves the radius. Measured on hh64-half-2-00004: Rdev 10.65 with p=4 against 10.32 with
    p=3, and the safe rim is 10.6 — so the old value would have returned a certificate computed on a
    develop bound too small for the tiling's own faces. The fix turns a false pass into an honest
    refusal, and the record renders correctly either way through the 2D path.
  ⚑ THE `certified` STAMPS HAD BEEN WIPED. emit_hyp_half_shelf.py writes records that have never carried
    the flag, so yesterday's emit (which added the {5,4} board) destroyed the 34-minute stamping run for
    {4,5} and {3,7}; I then stamped only the new board and reported the shelf as stamped. It went
    unnoticed for a day and was found by diffing against a backup taken before re-emitting today.
    scripts/stamp-hyp-half-parallel.mjs now exists — it pools every record across the 17 shards, which
    run from 4 rows to 15,443, so dealing whole shards to workers (what the hyp-poly driver does) leaves
    one worker holding most of the corpus. Re-run it after EVERY emit.
  ⚑ Neither half-tile shelf had a test at all — 23,372 shipped tilings, nothing checking any of them,
    which is how both of the above got through. lib/tilings/hyp-half.test.ts now guards this one and
    caught three of my own assertions being wrong about the data while I wrote it. The SPHERICAL half
    shelf is still untested.

